# nl-startup-1: owned discovery restoration

## Verified original contract

C:/Project/mixdog/src/standalone/daemon.mjs:639 installs watchOwnedFiles only after
claiming ownership. Lines 650-665 follow the owner lock and daemon.json.
Lines 802-828 republish when readDiscoveryAsync returns no matching pid; this
includes deletion, unreadable/malformed JSON, and another pid. A document naming
the same pid is left untouched even if its endpoints/other fields were replaced.
daemon-owner.mjs:8-10 uses Number(parsed?.pid) || 0, not a strict number-type test.
The shuttingDown guard disables republication. watched-file-cache.mjs:24 uses a
50ms debounce; daemon-owned-files.mjs::published explicitly rechecks a missing
post-write stamp so deletion immediately after publication is not missed.

The corrected Rust implementation uses notify events and the same 50ms debounce,
not the former 250ms polling. It follows the runtime root and nearest existing
ancestor, rebinds on changes, and immediately judges current content. Watch loss
is logged; there is no periodic fallback while blind, matching the original limit.
Every successful publication also enqueues a recheck. Drop/release serialize with
an in-flight recheck, clear the retained document, and stop/prevent resurrection.

Ownership is tied to the actual startup transition: startup.rs:152-171 does not
publish migrating until GateStep::Clear. boot.rs:88 forwards that state. Thus only
Migrating/Serving documents are retained for automatic publication; Booting and
Blocked still publish once under the existing lifecycle contract, but cannot
compete with the owner. Draining disarms the watcher before its fallible write.
The existing fencing_ownership durability-barrier design remains separate.

